 "pattern": "Track the lowest price so far",
 "statement": "`prices[i]` is a stock's price on day `i`. You may buy on one day and sell on a **later** day. Return the largest profit possible, or 0 if no profit can be made.",
 "example": {
  "input": "prices = [7,1,5,3,6,4]",
  "output": "5",
  "why": "Buy on day 1 at price 1 and sell on day 4 at price 6: 6 − 1 = 5."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "prices = [7,6,4,3,1]",
   "output": "0"
  },
  {
   "input": "prices = [2,4,1]",
   "output": "2"
  },
  {
   "input": "prices = []",
   "output": "0"
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "Trying every (buy day, sell day) pair is O(n²).",
  "If we sell today, the best day to have bought is simply the **cheapest day before today**.",
  "So walk through the days once, remembering the lowest price seen so far. Each day, check the profit of selling today and keep the best."
 ],
 "code": "class Solution:\n    def maxProfit(self, prices: List[int]) -> int:\n        if not prices:\n            return 0\n\n        min_price = float(\"inf\")\n        max_profit = 0\n\n        for price in prices:\n            min_price = min(min_price, price)\n            max_profit = max(max_profit, price - min_price)\n\n        return max_profit",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(n)",
  "timeWhy": "One pass over the prices.",
  "space": "O(1)",
  "spaceWhy": "Only the lowest price and the best profit are stored."
 },
